{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-01",
  "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B023",
        "laureates": [
          "Michael V. Berry",
          "Yakir Aharonov"
        ],
        "citation": "for revealing the observable geometric and gauge structure of quantum phase"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B025",
    "B010"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "B023 remains my preference. The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase established that quantum phase has observable physical content beyond a local dynamical phase. Their long experimental record and their consequences in quantum and condensed-matter physics make this an unusually mature conceptual achievement. Berry and Aharonov provide a compact attribution for the two foundational pillars, provided that the citation explicitly frames their common physical theme rather than implying identical historical priority.",
    "case_against": "The strongest reservation is that the Aharonov-Bohm effect and the Berry phase are historically distinct advances. A single prize can be defensible only if its formulation is tightly restricted to the physical observability of gauge and geometric phase; otherwise the pairing risks blurring contributors and histories. B025 and B010 have especially strong maturity, but each also requires a more difficult reconstruction of distributed foundational credit.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson notes that B023 combines two distinct discoveries and may therefore obscure their separate histories and contributors.",
        "response": "I agree with the qualification, but not with treating it as disqualifying. The common theme is sufficiently precise: both advances made global phase structure physically measurable. The citation should avoid claiming that one result is merely an extension of the other, and historical review should test whether the two-person allocation remains fair under that narrow scope."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that B052 uniquely joins foundational theory with decisive quantitative experimental confirmation in a mature universality class.",
        "response": "That is a strong case for B052, particularly its theory-experiment connection. I nevertheless give B023 priority because its experimentally established insight has reshaped the basic interpretation of quantum mechanics and has become a working foundation across multiple physical domains. The appropriate relative recognition of the KPZ theorists and later experimental confirmation remains an unresolved attribution question."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes the exceptional reproducibility, precision, and scientific utility of optical lattice clocks in B056.",
        "response": "The clocks are an exemplary mature achievement with unusually clear attribution to Katori and Ye. My reservation is not about their performance but about whether the proposed achievement has yet separated its long-term fundamental scientific consequences from its remarkable metrological advance as clearly as B023 has."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a sufficiently narrow historical and scientific framing can unite the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase without unfairly excluding essential contributors.",
      "Whether B025's three named architects can be distinguished adequately from the wider collaborative development of dynamical mean-field theory."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
